When a user profile attribute is configured it is still possible to give it a multi-valued string. For example, if an attribute named `age` is defined the following are all valid representations of that attribute on the user:

```json
{
  "attributes": { "age": ["18"] }
}
```
```json
{
  "attributes": { "age": ["18", "42"] }
}
```

This makes the value of the attribute extremely ambiguous for whomever is consuming this data from the REST API, making it had to reason about. One cannot make assumptions about which of these values is correct, allowing room for bugs to be introduced.

Therefore the proposal is as follows:
- Add a boolean property called `isMultiValued` to [`UserProfileAttribute`](https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/22123e0afc39935a88306deb816d0146519bf21e/services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/userprofile/config/UPAttribute.java#L28), defaulting to `false`.
- If  `isMultiValued` is `true` an array of multiple strings is accepted as a value (e.g. `"age": ["18", "42"]`).
- If `isMultiValued` is `false` an array of a single string accepted as a value (e.g. `"age": ["18"]`).
- A checkbox will be added to the form in the admin console to toggle the value of `isMultiValued`.

### Description

Hi all.

In a current project, we need Keycloak to issue access tokens that adhere to a specific format imposed by an external system. More precisely, this system requires access tokens to include certain information, which one would rather expect to be represented as claims, in token scopes. Example excerpt for the expected token format:

```json
...

  "scope": "openid email profile manage_my_profile:FIXED_VALUE customer_id:USER_ATTRIBUTE_VALUE",
  "email_verified": true,
  "preferred_username": "test@example.org",
  "locale": "de",
  "email": "test@example.org"

...
```

In this example, `manage_my_profile:USER_INDEPENDENT_FIXED_VALUE` and `customer_id:USER_ATTRIBUTE_VALUE` are "claim-like" additions to the token scope. Here, `manage_my_profile` has a fixed value assigned that is independent of the user for whom the token got assigned. By contrast, `customer_id` requires the assigned value to denote the token user's customer ID, which is managed by the external system and that we store in a corresponding Keycloak user attribute for project-specific reasons.

While we can configure `manage_my_profile:USER_INDEPENDENT_FIXED_VALUE` as a Keycloak client scope as-is and let Keycloak add it to the token scope by default, we haven't found a way to create client scopes that (i) wrap Keycloak user attribute values in claims; and (ii) get added to token scopes rather than token claims.

In case there already exists an approach to extend token scopes with "claim-like" additions whose values stem from Keycloak user attributes, we would be glad to learn about it :-) However, since we weren't able to find such an approach, we implemented a custom protocol mapper based on upstream [`UserAttributeMapper`](https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/main/services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/protocol/oidc/mappers/UserAttributeMapper.java) and called it `UserAttributeToScopeMapper`. It behaves similar to `UserAttributeMapper` (for access tokens and access token responses), but adds user attribute claims to token scopes instead of token claims.

We now wonder whether adding user attribute claims to token scopes is a recurrent requirement in the wild and whether it could make sense to include this functionality in Keycloak itself? For reference purposes, I added the source code of our `UserAttributeToScopeMapper` to this issue's details and (in case its functionality is considered sensible for inclusion in upstream Keycloak) would volunteer to turn it into a Keycloak PR, e.g., (i) in its current or improved form as a dedicated class; (ii) as an extension to the upstream `UserAttributeMapper` where one could tick a checkbox to have the resulting claim added to a token's scope; or (iii) in any other form that conforms with Keycloak's design and implementation philosophy :-)

Looking forward to the discussion (or already existing upstream solutions to our requirement that we simply have overlooked ;-)).

Thanks,
Florian

