### Description

Currently Keycloak supports only the possibility to transform an access or ID token using `OIDCIDTokenMapper`. Using the UI you may configure _token mappers_ that allow to set claims, etc. in the generated access token. However, this is not possible for refresh tokens.

Our current project's specification mandates us to influence the way a refresh token is created upon a token exchange request (to set some claims, e.g.).
Thus, we suggest to provide an analogous mechanism to `OIDCIDTokenMapper` by introducing a `OIDCRefreshTokenMapper`.

The concrete reason is that currently there is no way to influence the claims of a refresh token generated by the `TokenManager.AccessTokenResponseBuilder` class. 

**To give an example:**

I know that you can configure global values for a realm for expiration dates of tokens. But what we need to do is to set the expiration (i.e. "exp") programmatically to a **_dynamic_** value which is defined by some rule engine. 

### Goals

We suggest to introduce a new interface `OIDCRefreshTokenMapper`:

```
public interface OIDCRefreshTokenMapper {
  RefreshToken transformRefreshToken(
      RefreshToken token, ProtocolMapperModel mappingModel, KeycloakSession session,
      UserSessionModel userSession, ClientSessionContext clientSession
  );
}
```

and use it in the `TokenManager` class:

```
public AccessTokenResponseBuilder generateRefreshToken(...) {
            ...
            transformRefreshToken();

            return this;
        }

```
```
private void transformRefreshToken() {
      ProtocolMapperUtils.getSortedProtocolMappers(session, clientSessionCtx)
          .filter(entry -> entry.getValue() instanceof OIDCRefreshTokenMapper)
          .forEach(entry -> {
                var mapper = (OIDCRefreshTokenMapper) entry.getValue();

                refreshToken = mapper.transformRefreshToken(refreshToken, entry.getKey(), session, userSession, clientSessionCtx);
              }
          );
    }
```

### Value Proposition

The feature wold close a logical gap that exists for some time. This will give us the necessary flexibility and allow for future extensions, such as custom claims in refresh tokens.

