**Author:** @embesozzi
**Status:** Draft
**Type:** Feature Proposal
**Created:** 2026-04-06

## Summary
Its a common authorization pattern externalize policy enforcement point at the gateway level. 
In this case when an API gateway calls the AuthZEN API evaluation endpoint its using their own service account token. With current implementation keycloak always evaluates policies against the resource server bound to the token `azp` claim (issuer for).

This makes not possible to the gateway to evaluate other client resource server and the only workaround is to model all the policies with the context of the client gateway.

This issue proposes a minimal extension to evaluateSingle() that allows explicit resource server selection, enabling gateway clients to perform cross-client policy evaluation.


### Value Proposition

This feature allows the gateway to evaluate against any application client's own resources, so each application team owns and manages their policies independently while the gateway remains a neutral enforcer. 
No duplication, no coupling, no coordination overhead as applications are added or removed.


### Goals

- Enable policy ownership at the application level
- Keep the gateway as a neutral enforcer
- Zero duplication: removing the requirement to mirror resources, scopes, and policies from application clients into the gateway client.
- Backward compatible: when resource_server_client_id is absent the endpoint behaves exactly as today; no existing deployments are affected.

### Discussion

_No response_

### Notes

####   Current Behavior

`AuthZenResource.evaluateSingle() `resolves the resource server exclusively from the authenticating token's issued_for claim:

In a multi-application deployment:

Realm: demo
    |_ demo-client     → RS (policies, resources, scopes)
    |_ billing-client     → RS (policies, resources, scopes)
    |_ gateway-client → must mirror ALL of the above to work as a PEP

The gateway must duplicate each apps policies.

#### Proposed Behavior

Allow the caller to specify the target resource server in the AuthZEN request for example:

```
{
    "subject":  { "type": "user", "id": "alice" },
    "resource": {
      "id":   "todo-1",
      "type": "todo",
      "properties": {
        "resource_server_client_id": "demo-client"
      }
    },
    "action": { "name": "can_read_todos" }
  } 

```
---
#### Trust Model

The trust signal is the presence of an entry for the target client in the caller's resource_access claim:

```
 {
    "resource_access": {
      "demo-client": {
        "roles": ["authzen-evaluate"]
      }
    }
  }
```

The role name is arbitrary; its only purpose is to get an entry into resource_access.<demo-client>.

Note: I also considered using the `aud` claim or FGAP policies to verify whether the caller is authorized. FGAP could be a valid option as well, but for this initial implementation it would add unnecessary complexity to the authorization logic.


Proposed Change to evaluateSingle()

  ```java
String targetClientId = request.resource().properties() != null
      ? (String) request.resource().properties().get("resource_server_client_id")
      : null;

  ClientModel client;
  if (targetClientId != null) {
      ClientModel targetClient = getClientByClientId(targetClientId, realm);
      if (targetClient == null)
          throw new ErrorResponseException("invalid_target",
              "Target client not found", Status.BAD_REQUEST);

      // Trust check: calling SA must have any role from the target client
      // (resource_access.<targetClientId> present in token)
      AccessToken.Access targetAccess = token.getResourceAccess(targetClientId);
      if (targetAccess == null || targetAccess.getRoles().isEmpty())
          throw new ErrorResponseException("access_denied",
              "Caller has no resource_access entry for target client", Status.FORBIDDEN);

      client = targetClient;
  } else {
      client = getClientByClientId(token.getIssuedFor(), realm);
  }

  ResourceServer resourceServer = storeFactory.getResourceServerStore().findByClient(client);
```
